   "text": {
    "L1": "A change can be physical or chemical. The key question is: has a new substance formed?",
    "L2": "In a physical change, shape, size or state changes, but no new substance forms.",
    "L3": "Melting ghee is physical. It is still ghee, and it becomes solid again when cooled.",
    "L4": "Dissolving is also physical. Sugar spreads into water, but it is still there and still has mass.",
    "L5": "In a chemical change, one or more new substances with different properties are formed.",
    "L6": "Gas bubbles, a colour change, heat or light, a new smell, or a new solid can be evidence.",
    "L7": "Here is a common myth: dissolved sugar has disappeared and has no mass. Not true; dissolving does not make the sugar vanish.",
    "L8": "Baking soda and lemon juice fizz because a new gas forms. That is a chemical change."
   },
